 `make bootstrap` is safe to repeat and uses `go.mod`, `go.sum`, and `pnpm-lock.yaml`. `make dev` waits up to four minutes for the full dependency chain. `make smoke` expects an already-running stack and verifies the embedded Web surface, API, trace IDs, migration completion, worker persistence, PostgreSQL failure and recovery, and shutdown persistence. The smoke journey is disruptive: it briefly stops PostgreSQL and recreates this checkout's containers and network while preserving the data volume. Never run it against a shared project identity.
 
+## Local Governance Acceptance
+
+The Compose application exposes a development-only three-person acceptance flow. Open the HTTP port recorded in `.semlia/dev.env`; the top bar shows the active local identity. The complete product navigation remains available. The local acceptance claim applies to the real Catalog and Governance surfaces; other surfaces retain their own preview or session-data disclosures.
+
+1. Select `提案发起`, open `知识资产`, create or open an asset, and submit a knowledge revision.
+2. Wait for the proposal to reach `待审核`, select `独立评审`, open `变更与发布`, and approve the candidate with a review note.
+3. Select `独立发布`, reopen the candidate, and publish it. Refresh the browser and verify that the approval and release record remain visible.
+4. Exercise a refusal by trying to review as the proposal author or publish as the reviewer; the service returns a separation-of-duty error and writes an audit fact.
+
+The aliases `local-author`, `local-reviewer`, and `local-publisher` are enabled only by `SEMLIA_LOCAL_UAT_IDENTITIES=true` in the development Compose server. They are not authentication and are invalid outside the development environment. This flow supports local product acceptance, not external or production user testing.
+
+## Controlled-User OIDC Mode
+
+Controlled-user environments use a generic OpenID Connect provider with Authorization Code and
+PKCE. The server discovers the provider metadata from `SEMLIA_OIDC_ISSUER`, validates the ID token's
+issuer, audience and signature, and maps the stable issuer/subject pair to a global account. A
+verified email is used only to match an outstanding invitation; it never remaps an established
+external identity.
+
+Configure the server with:
+
+```dotenv
+SEMLIA_AUTH_MODE=oidc
+SEMLIA_OIDC_ISSUER=https://identity.example.com
+SEMLIA_OIDC_CLIENT_ID=semlia
+SEMLIA_OIDC_CLIENT_SECRET=replace-with-provider-secret
+SEMLIA_OIDC_REDIRECT_URL=https://semlia.example.com/api/v1/auth/callback
+SEMLIA_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=https://semlia.example.com
+SEMLIA_SECRET_KEY=replace-with-at-least-32-random-bytes
+```
+
+Production mode requires HTTPS origins, a TLS-enabled PostgreSQL URL, OIDC configuration and the
+application secret. The provider secret, application secret, session token, CSRF verifier and PKCE
+verifier must not be written to logs or stored in plaintext.
+
+After applying migrations, create the first workspace and administrator invitation once. The
+command is idempotent for the same issuer, workspace slug and normalized email:
+
+```bash
+./build/semlia bootstrap-admin semantic-core "Semantic Core" founder@example.com
+```
+
+The invited person signs in through `/api/v1/auth/login`. Admission occurs only when the verified
+OIDC identity matches the pending issuer/subject or issuer/email invitation. Sessions are opaque,
+stored by digest in PostgreSQL and revoked immediately on logout. Unsafe cookie-authenticated API
+requests require both an allowlisted `Origin` and the `X-Semlia-CSRF` value returned in the
+`GET /api/v1/session` response header.
+
+## Protected PostgreSQL Sources
+
+Source credentials enter Semlia only through source creation or credential rotation. The API never
+returns a password, connection URI, encrypted value or nonce. PostgreSQL metadata collection pins
+the credential version when the discovery run is queued, rejects elevated or schema-writable roles,
+opens a read-only transaction, and reads only catalogs for relations, fields, keys and foreign keys.
+
+Optional versioned `.sql` evidence is resolved below `SEMLIA_SOURCE_ARTIFACT_ROOT`. Paths are
+relative, must end in `.sql`, and cannot traverse the root or use symbolic links. The worker needs
+read access to that root; the server only validates and stores the relative path list.
+
+Use a dedicated PostgreSQL login with only `CONNECT`, schema `USAGE`, and `SELECT` on the intended
+relations. Do not reuse the Semlia application database owner. A queued run continues with its
+pinned credential version if the source credential is rotated before the worker claims the job.
+
 Inspect state and logs with:
 
 ```bash
